  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 2,
    "lesson_title": "Phase Response",
    "lesson_core_move": "Interpret $\\angle$ $H(j\\omega)$ as the frequency-dependent phase shift applied to each sinusoidal input component when using frequency response to characterize LTI filter behavior.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Phase Response",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Phase Response",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Phase response is the phase part of the frequency response. For a sinusoidal component at frequency $\\omega_0$, $\\angle H(j\\omega_0)$ is the phase shift the system adds. It changes timing, not amplitude.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=A\\cos(\\omega_0 t+\\phi)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use a single cosine input $x(t)=A\\cos(\\omega_0 t+\\phi)$ so only one frequency and one phase adjustment are in play. The system keeps the same sinusoid frequency, scales the amplitude by $|H(j\\omega_0)|$, and adds the system phase to the input phase.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=A|H(j\\omega_0)|\\cos\\big(\\omega_0 t+\\phi+\\angle H(j\\omega_0)\\big)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "With this sign convention, positive $\\angle H(j\\omega_0)$ advances the sinusoid and negative $\\angle H(j\\omega_0)$ delays it. Later examples will use that timing language directly when they read phase response.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\text{phase shift at }\\omega_0=\\angle H(j\\omega_0)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Phase Response",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Reading $\\angle$ $H(j\\omega)$ at a Single Frequency",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Given $H(j4)=3e^{j\\pi/4}$, identify the phase shift experienced by a sinusoidal input at $\\omega=4$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the single frequency $\\omega=4$, and the response is already written in polar form as $H(j4)=3e^{j\\pi/4}$. For phase response, read the angle, not the magnitude, because the angle is the phase shift at that frequency.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "H(j4)=3e^{j\\pi/4}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The argument of $H(j4)$ is $\\pi/4$, so the phase shift at $\\omega=4$ is $\\pi/4$. In lead-or-lag language, this is a phase lead of $\\pi/4$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\angle H(j4)=\\pi/4",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Phase Response",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Applying Phase Response to a Sinusoidal Component",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "If $x(t)=\\cos(2t+\\pi/4)$ and $\\angle H(j2)=-\\pi/6$, write the steady-state output phase for the sinusoidal component.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is a single sinusoid at $\\omega_0=2$: $x(t)=\\cos(2t+\\pi/4)$. Read the phase response at the same frequency, $\\angle H(j2)=-\\pi/6$, and keep the frequency fixed while you add the system phase to the input phase.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=\\cos(2t+\\pi/4)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Add the phases: $\\pi/4+(-\\pi/6)=\\pi/12$. So the steady-state output keeps the same frequency and uses the new phase $\\pi/12$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=\\cos(2t+\\pi/12)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Phase Response",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Interpreting Positive and Negative Phase Shifts",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "If $\\angle H(j3)=\\pi/5$ and $\\angle H(j8)=-\\pi/5$, describe the phase change at each frequency in terms of lead or lag.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the sign of $\\angle H(j\\omega)$ at each frequency. A positive phase value means the output leads, or advances, the input. A negative phase value means the output lags, or delays, the input.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\angle H(j3)=\\pi/5, \\qquad \\angle H(j8)=-\\pi/5",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So at $\\omega=3$, the output leads the input by $\\pi/5$. At $\\omega=8$, the output lags the input by $\\pi/5$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\omega=3:\\ \\text{lead by }\\pi/5 \\qquad \\omega=8:\\ \\text{lag by }\\pi/5",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Phase Response",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Comparing Phase Shifts Across Frequencies",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "If $\\angle H(j1)=\\pi/6$ and $\\angle H(j5)=-\\pi/3$, determine which frequency component is more delayed and which is more advanced.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Read each phase shift first. At $\\omega=1$, the phase is positive, so that component is advanced. At $\\omega=5$, the phase is negative, so that component is delayed.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\angle H(j1)=\\pi/6, \\qquad \\angle H(j5)=-\\pi/3",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now compare the sizes: $|\\pi/6| < |\\pi/3|$, so the delay at $\\omega=5$ is larger in magnitude than the advance at $\\omega=1$. The $\\omega=5$ component is more delayed, and the $\\omega=1$ component is more advanced.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\omega=1:\\ \\text{advanced by }\\pi/6 \\qquad \\omega=5:\\ \\text{delayed by }\\pi/3",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
